{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>He has not beheld iniquity in Jacob.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): there is no iniquity in them — for were there, the good would not be fulfilled in them, since all the words of the Blessed Name are conditional. And because there is no iniquity or trouble in them, ״HaShem his Elohim is with him״ — the Shekhinah is among them — ״and the shout of a king is in him,״ for in the camp of Israel, in all their journeys, they sound a teru'ah, for so is the custom of the king's legions." "<b>And I have heard</b> that Balaam needed to mention in his blessing the word 'king' (מלך), because the curse — had it not been reversed — would have been כל״ם at that very moment, which was the hour of wrath. And the Holy One, blessed be He, who placed the matter in his mouth, put it into his heart to reverse the word that was כל״ם and say מלך. And this is what is written (Deuteronomy 23:6): ״ויהפוך ה׳ לך את הקללה לברכה״ — 'and HaShem turned for you the curse into a blessing.'" "<b>And there are</b> commentators who explain: when is ״HaShem his Elohim with him״? — ״when the shout of a king is in him,״ when there is among them attachment (דביקות) with the King. And it is from the root of רעות (companionship/desire), for then they are above the stars." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: ״HaShem his Elohim is with him״ and ״the shout of a king is in him״ — because the exodus from Egypt was בכח גדול וביד חזקה, and this is the matter of which Moses said (Exodus 32:11): ״אשר הוצאת מארץ מצרים בכח גדול וביד חזקה״ — 'whom You brought out from the land of Egypt with great power and with a strong hand.' Therefore he mentioned here ״HaShem his Elohim is with him״ — HaShem, which is His Elohim, and this is כח גדול." "<b>And the shout of a king is in him.</b> This is יד חזקה, and therefore it is immediately followed by mention of Him who brought them out of Egypt. And this is what Onkelos translated: ושכינת מלכהון — 'and the Shekhinah of their King.' And it is from the root of רעיא (the Beloved), and she is the Bride in the Song of Songs, and this is clear."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {1 "The Hebrew acronym כל״ם (kuf-lamed-mem) is rendered transliterated, as its resolution is uncertain in context."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:35:36.338663947Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}